  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 8,456 feet, <strong>Crane Mountain</strong> is a summit in Oregon. The Crane Mountain Trail covers 26.0 miles with about 2,835 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>The grade is back-loaded: the first half of the route climbs gently, and most of the elevation gain comes in the second half. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 24.17, gaining roughly 500 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 7,014 feet, the route tops out near 8,419 feet. You'll cross above treeline on the way up, so the upper route is exposed to weather \u2014 check the forecast and turn around if conditions build.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=42.31658,-120.16798\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">42.31658, -120.16798</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 195 miles north of Reno, Nevada. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>Most hikers should budget about 10.0 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Light Peak, Drake Peak and Gearhart Mountain \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
